/*******************************************************************************
                    What Is A Directive
*******************************************************************************/
    An incant directive is a small named field whose sub-fields describe a
    single edit to apply to a text buffer loaded via getFile. Three actions
    consume them:

        deleteAt(directive)    remove matched text
        insertAt(directive)    insert text before or after a matched line
        replaceAt(directive)   replace matched text in-place

    Directive sub-fields:
        source     field loaded via getFile — the buffer being edited
        fromThis   text to match (any substring, not just line-start)
        toThis     replacement or insertion text; may be a single token or a
                   large multi-line block (quote with \n for newlines).
                   deleteAt does not take one
        where      insertAt only — "before" inserts ahead of the matched line;
                   any other value (or omitted) inserts after it

    Advantages over tok directives:
        - fromThis matches anywhere on a line; tok matches line-start only
        - toThis can be arbitrarily large — swap a single token or an entire
          set of actions for a revised set in one directive
        - deleting is its own verb, so a directive never has to be read for
          what it LACKS

    These actions mutate source's in-memory buffer via the mark-aware buffer
    operators: IN finds fromThis and sets the mark; -= removes at the mark;
    += inserts at the mark. The mark only advances — it never moves backward.
    Directives on one buffer must therefore target locations in forward
    (top-to-bottom) order; a fromThis that falls behind the current mark will
    not match. getFile resets the mark to the buffer start.

    See APPLYING DIRECTIVES and APPLYING A BATCH TO PRODUCTION SOURCE -- the certificate every batch owes
    A batch that edits committed source runs with git as its undo, not with the
    copy-to-Tests workflow. That is sound only if the batch is certified, and the
    certificate has a required clause:

    ⚠ SECOND-RUN-INERT IS A REQUIREMENT, NOT A NICETY. Run the batch a SECOND
    time. Every directive must refuse -- the count of refusals equals the count
    of calls -- and every edited file must come back md5-IDENTICAL. A batch that
    is right once and wrong twice is a batch nobody can re-run, and re-running is
    what happens when a batch is half-applied and someone reaches for it again.

    ⚠ IT IS NOT A FORMALITY, AND IT PAID ON THE FIRST BATCH EVER APPLIED. The
    2026-09-16 dotted-path sweep passed its first run with 30 of 30 keys correct
    and zero misses. Its SECOND run rewrote a BLOCK comment: one path appeared
    both as an inline key, which the sweep was for, and inside a `/* */` block,
    which the convention leaves alone. The first run consumed the inline one; the
    second found the block one and changed it. Nothing in the first run's output
    was wrong, so only the second run could have caught it.

    END-ANCHOR fromThis WITH \n FOR AN INLINE KEY. An inline key is always the
    last thing on its line and a block-comment mention never is, so the newline
    is the discriminator, and it makes the batch idempotent by CONSTRUCTION
    rather than by luck. Do not drop it because a particular path looks unique --
    it is a claim about every other occurrence in the file, which is the kind of
    claim nobody checks.

    ONE FIELD CAN REACH TWO FILES. getFile is once per FIELD, but a field can be
    RE-POINTED: closeFile(source), then assign the next path to source, then
    getFile(source) again. Measured 2026-09-16; it is how one batch edits both
    Commands.rtn and ruleActions.rtn in one run.
    ⚠ `source:=otherField` was tried first and exits 139. The source sub-field
    must be the bare reference to a field literally named `source`, so a batch
    spanning files re-points that ONE field between batches rather than naming a
    second one.

    THE REST OF THE CERTIFICATE, for a batch over .rtn source: codeOnly.py on
    every edited file byte-identical (the sweep is comment-only), a bare retok,
    the extern canary, codeOnly.py on the generated .mm byte-identical, a
    rebuild, and the fleet unmoved row for row.

    Batches live in genLadder/batches/ and are TRACKED. A batch that edited
    production and was then thrown away is a change nobody can audit.

KNOWN LIMITS in the usage guide below.

